# Routing a question about `{{ .RepoID }}`

Four kinds of question, four routes, and picking the wrong one is the usual
reason an answer is vague. Decide first, then call **one** tool, then widen only
if it comes back empty.

| The question is… | Route it to | Because |
|---|---|---|
| **Structured** — an id, a status, an assignee, a sprint, a parent, a label, a date range | `gintrack` MCP: `get_item`, `list_items` | These read the front matter directly. They are exact, they are cheap, and they return a `rev` you need for any write. |
| **Literal** — words you expect to appear verbatim in an item or a page | `search_items`, `search_kb` | Substring ranking over ids, titles, labels and bodies, straight out of the repository index. |
| **Semantic** — a topic, a decision, "where did we say…", wording the user half-remembers | `search_semantic` (fallback: `kb_search_documents` with a `path_prefix`) | It ranks items and pages by meaning and resolves every hit back to the live repository, so you get an id and a `rev`, not a mirror of one. |
| **Code** — a symbol, a call site, "how is X implemented" | `code_hybrid_search`, then `code_find_symbol` | The code index is the only one that sees Go, TypeScript and their symbol graph. The backlog corpus is Markdown only. |

The same table is in `docs/08-mcp-server.md` §4.19 and `docs/20-agent-interface.md`.

## One example per row

**Structured.** "Which tasks of GIT-US-0069 are still open?"

```
list_items(parent: "GIT-US-0069", status: ["todo", "in_progress"],
           fields: ["id", "title", "status", "assignees"])
```

Not `kb_search_documents("open tasks of GIT-US-0069")`: the status lives in front
matter, so an exact filter beats a similarity score every time.

**Literal.** "Which items mention `X-Pando-Token`?"

```
search_items(query: "X-Pando-Token")
```

The string appears verbatim, so substring ranking is exact and free. Reach for
`search_kb` for the same question over knowledge-base pages.

**Semantic.** "Where did we decide how the CORS proxy is allowed to forward?"

```
search_semantic(query: "CORS proxy allow-list git smart HTTP", limit: 5)
```

Hits come back with `kind`, `id` or `path`, `score`, a snippet and the current
`rev`, ranked by meaning rather than by wording. `kind: "item"` or `"page"`
narrows it; `project` scopes it to one project key.

If `search_semantic` answers `{"error":{"code":"unavailable"}}`, this session has
no Pando backend wired into gintrack. Do **not** report that nothing matched —
the query never ran. Fall back to `search_items` and `search_kb` with the words
you expect literally, or search the exported corpus directly:

```
kb_search_documents(query: "CORS proxy allow-list git smart HTTP",
                    path_prefix: "kb/")
```

`file_path` is relative to the corpus root, so the two prefixes that matter are
`items/` (one document per backlog item) and `kb/` (one per knowledge-base
page). An empty prefix searches every document Pando has indexed, this
repository's corpus included — that is rarely what you want.

Then read the winner with `kb_get_document` and, if it is an item, confirm it
with `get_item` — the exported copy can lag the repository by one export.

**Code.** "How does the companion decide which repositories to serve?"

```
code_hybrid_search(query: "workspace repos resolution companion serve")
code_find_symbol(name: "WorkspaceRepos")
```

## Prefer separate calls over one combined one

`hybrid_search_remembrances` searches memories, knowledge base and code at once
and returns a blended ranking. That is the wrong shape here: it hides which
index answered, it mixes Pando's own memories into a question about this
repository, and it takes no `path_prefix`. Call the specific tool, and call the
second one only when the first returns nothing.

## After a semantic hit, go back to the source

`search_semantic` and `kb_search_documents` return **candidates**. A snippet is a
mirror; the repository is the authority. Take the item id out of the hit, call
`get_item`, and answer from that. Cite the id, not the snippet.

## Nothing found

Say so. Name the tool you used and the query you used, and offer the next
narrower or wider query. Do not guess an id, and do not answer from memory of
another repository.
